const chartCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '10 + 1.5*sin(x/3) + 0.8*sin(3*x)', label: 'sample mean (in control)', color: '#0f766e', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '14.5 + 0*x', label: 'UCL (+3σ)', color: '#b42318', width: 1.4 },
    { expr: '5.5 + 0*x', label: 'LCL (−3σ)', color: '#b42318', width: 1.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'sample number' },
  y: { min: 3, max: 17, label: 'measurement' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const spcCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · All processes vary', note: 'Two sources: common-cause variation (the process\'s natural noise) and special-cause variation (something changed).' },
    { math: '2 · Control limits ≠ spec limits', note: 'Control limits come from the process\'s own data (±3σ); specification limits come from the customer. A process can be in control and still make bad parts.' },
    { math: '3 · The control chart listens', note: 'Plot sample statistics over time with ±3σ limits. Points inside, randomly scattered: common cause only. React to anything else.' },
    { math: '4 · Patterns are signals', note: 'A run of 8 on one side, a trend, a cycle — each is a special-cause signal worth hunting, even inside the limits.' },
    { math: '5 · Improve, then re-baseline', note: 'After eliminating special causes, reduce common-cause variation itself — that is process improvement, and the chart re-baselines tighter.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/industrial-engineering/foundational/statistics-quality-and-control-charts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Industrial engineering begins with a statistical insight that transformed manufacturing:
    <strong>variation is not an enemy to eliminate but a signal to interpret</strong>. Every
    process — a filling line, a machining cell, an insurance claim queue — produces outputs that
    wobble. Some of the wobble is the process's natural breathing (common cause); some of it is
    something that changed and should be hunted down (special cause). Walter Shewhart's control
    chart, invented at Bell Laboratories in 1924, separated the two with a simple statistical
    device, and that single distinction — applied relentlessly — is how the modern quality
    profession was built. Its consequences reach from the factory floor to Six Sigma, and the
    logic underneath is pure statistics: distributions, sampling and inference.
  </p>
  <p>
    To see why statistics is the right tool, consider the fill line example this page will keep
    returning to: a machine filling bottles nominally to 500 mL. Every bottle differs slightly —
    a millilitre up here, one down there. If you measure one bottle and it comes out at 503 mL,
    is the machine drifting out of adjustment, or was that bottle just a naturally high one?
    One data point cannot answer; the question only becomes decidable when the variation's
    own statistics are known. That is the whole programme: characterise the noise, then let
    the noise define what counts as a signal.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Shewhart's insight: common vs special causes" widget="equationStep" config={spcCfg} />

  <h2>Distributions and inference: the machinery underneath</h2>
  <p>
    Quality statistics is built on a small set of distributions, each matching a kind of
    variation. The <strong>normal distribution</strong> (the bell curve, defined by mean μ and
    standard deviation σ) models continuous measurements — fill volumes, dimensions, weights —
    and its celebrated property is area-based: about 68 per cent of output falls within ±1σ of
    the mean, 95 per cent within ±2σ, 99.7 per cent within ±3σ. Those area fractions are the
    control chart's calibration, and they are also the language of capability ("±3σ process",
    "six sigma" — literally a specification six standard deviations wide). Counts of defects or
    defectives follow the <strong>binomial</strong> (fraction nonconforming) and
    <strong>Poisson</strong> (defects per unit, rare events) distributions, which is why
    attribute control charts use different formulas for their limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bridge from distributions to decisions is <strong>inference</strong>, and its central
    pillar is the central limit theorem: even if individual bottles vary according to some
    lopsided, ugly distribution, the <em>averages of small samples</em> from that process pile
    up nearly normally around μ with a narrower spread — σ divided by the square root of the
    sample size. Averages stabilise; individuals do not. This is why quality work samples in
    small groups (subgroups of four or five, drawn close together in time) and charts the
    subgroup mean: the chart inherits the theorem's stability. Inference then supplies the
    decision grammar. A control chart is a running hypothesis test — the null hypothesis is
    "the process is unchanged" — and it therefore lives with the two classic errors:
    the <strong>Type I error</strong> (a false alarm: reacting to pure noise, ~0.27 per cent
    per point with ±3σ limits — roughly one false alarm per 370 points) and the
    <strong>Type II error</strong> (missing a real shift because it is gradual or small).
    Shewhart's choice of ±3σ limits is precisely a pricing of those two risks; the design of
    every chart is a wager on which error the process can better afford.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a process in statistical control" widget="plotter" config={chartCfg} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a point outside the control limits "proves" the process failed. The limits are a 99.7 per cent rule, not a law of nature — roughly one point in 370 crosses them by chance alone, which is why a single excursion prompts investigation (was anything special going on?), not automatic verdicts. Statistics can only say "this pattern would be very unlikely if nothing changed" — the special cause still has to be found by a human who knows the process.</p>

  <h2>Statistical process control: the chart as conversation</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>control chart</strong> operationalises the common/special distinction. Take
    subgroups of, say, n = 5 consecutive parts every hour; compute each subgroup's mean and
    range; plot them on an X̄ chart and an R chart with limits computed <em>from the process's
    own data</em> — not from any specification. The upper and lower control limits sit at
    ±3σ of the plotted statistic, where σ is estimated from the observed subgroup variation.
    The plot above shows a process in statistical control: points scattered randomly within
    the limits, the process talking about itself. The chart's discipline is its restraint:
    <em>react only to signals</em>. A point beyond a limit is a signal; so are subtler
    patterns — the Western Electric run rules (two of three points beyond 2σ on one side; a
    run of eight on one side of the centre line; six points steadily trending; fourteen
    alternating), each of which is very unlikely under pure randomness. Each signal says the
    same thing in different words: <em>something changed — go find it</em>, a new operator, a
    dull tool, a different raw-material lot, a drifting sensor.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two disciplines make SPC work, and both are cultural as much as mathematical.
    <strong>Rational subgrouping</strong>: the subgroup must be sampled so that only common
    causes can act <em>within</em> it (consecutive parts), while the opportunities for special
    causes lie <em>between</em> subgroups — otherwise the limits become meaningless. And
    <strong>restraint against tampering</strong>: the gravest error in practice is reacting to
    common-cause noise — adjusting a stable machine after every high reading. Deming's funnel
    experiment demonstrates the result: chasing noise with adjustments makes the output's
    variation <em>larger</em>, not smaller. "Do not adjust a process in statistical control"
    is counter-intuitive, which is exactly why the chart exists: to tell the operator when
    restraint, not action, is the professional move. Charts also come in families for
    attributes — the p-chart for fraction nonconforming, the c-chart for defects per unit —
    extending the same logic to counts and pass/fail data, and modern extensions (CUSUM and
    EWMA charts) accumulate evidence across points to catch the small, slow shifts that
    Shewhart charts miss.
  </p>

  <h2>Capability and acceptance sampling: meeting the customer, lot by lot</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Control limits are not specification limits</strong>, and the distinction is the
    hinge of the whole field. Control limits are the process's voice (its own variation);
    specification limits are the customer's voice (what the part must satisfy). A process can
    be perfectly in control and perfectly out of specification — tightly and consistently
    producing bad parts. <strong>Capability indices</strong> compare the two voices. Cp is the
    specification width divided by the process width (6σ); Cpk adds a penalty for being off
    centre. Worked example: a fill specification of 500 ± 2 mL with process σ = 0.5 mL gives
    Cp = 4/3 ≈ 1.33 — the classical "capable" benchmark; if the process mean has drifted to
    500.4 mL, Cpk = min((502 − 500.4)/1.5, (500.4 − 498)/1.5) ≈ 1.07 — the same variation,
    but less of it usable. The ordering of work follows: achieve statistical control
    <em>first</em> (improving an unstable process is measuring fog), then centre it, then shrink
    σ until capability is comfortable. Control first, capability second.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Acceptance sampling</strong> is the statistical grandparent of the field and its
    cautionary tale: inspect a sample from each lot, and accept or reject the whole lot on the
    sample's evidence. Its grammar is the <strong>operating characteristic (OC) curve</strong>:
    for a given sampling plan (sample size n, acceptance number c), the curve shows the
    probability of accepting a lot as a function of the true defect rate. The vocabulary is
    two-sided risk: the <strong>producer's risk</strong> (α: the chance a good lot — at the
    acceptable quality level, AQL — is wrongly rejected) and the <strong>consumer's risk</strong>
    (β: the chance a bad lot — at the lot tolerance percent defective, LTPD — is wrongly
    accepted). Worked example with the Poisson approximation: plan n = 50, c = 1. At a true
    defect rate of 1 per cent, np = 0.5, and the acceptance probability is e<sup>−0.5</sup>(1
    + 0.5) ≈ 0.91 — good lots usually pass. At 4 per cent defective, np = 2, and the
    probability falls to e<sup>−2</sup>(1 + 2) ≈ 0.20 — bad lots usually fail. The whole plan
    is priced by that curve, and tables (MIL-STD-105E and its successor ANSI/ASQ Z1.4;
    Dodge–Romig plans) are nothing but pre-computed OC curves. The deeper lesson, though, is
    Deming's: sampling accepts quality <em>after the fact</em>, while SPC builds it
    <em>in</em> — inspecting quality in is expensive and never as good as making the process
    incapable of producing defects, which is why mature industries treat sampling plans as a
    floor for incoming material, not a quality strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One more worked passage shows the attribute-chart half of SPC, because most real data
    begins as pass/fail rather than measurements. A hospital laboratory tracks its specimen
    rejection rate: each day it receives n = 200 tubes and records how many arrive clotted,
    mislabelled or haemolysed. The p-chart plots the daily fraction rejected, with limits set
    by the binomial logic: if the long-run rate is 4 per cent, the centre line is 0.04, the
    standard error is √(0.04 × 0.96 / 200) ≈ 0.014, and three-sigma limits sit at
    0.04 ± 3 × 0.014 — from effectively zero up to about 8.2 per cent. A day at 9 per cent
    rejected fires the upper limit; a week at zero rejections eventually fires the lower one — and the lower signal is
    as valuable as the upper, because "no rejects all week" means something changed: a new
    courier, a retrained phlebotomy team, or (the sceptic's hypothesis) a mis-booked day.
    Attribute charts thus extend Shewhart's grammar to counts, rates and proportions, and
    they carry the same discipline: limits from the process's own voice, restraint inside
    them, investigation outside.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three currents carry this material into modern practice. <strong>Measurement system
    analysis</strong>: before any chart or capability index, the measurement itself must be
    validated — a Gage R&R study partitions observed variation into part-to-part, operator and
    instrument components, and the field's rule of thumb is that measurement variation should
    consume well under a tenth of tolerance; charting a process through a bad gauge is
    measuring fog with a fog machine. <strong>The philosophy layer</strong>: Shewhart's chart
    grew into Deming's management system (variation thinking applied to whole organisations —
    most quality problems are common cause, and blaming individuals for them is a category
    error), and into Taguchi's <strong>loss function</strong>, which replaced the step-function
    view of quality ("inside spec = fine, outside = bad") with a quadratic: any deviation from
    target costs money, proportionally to its square — which reframes capability as an economic
    quantity, not a pass/fail one. <strong>The data layer</strong>: Six Sigma's DMAIC cycle
    industrialised the inferential toolkit (measure, analyse, improve, control) around
    millions of dollars of projects; and Industry 4.0 is turning SPC inside out — in-line
    sensors measuring every unit instead of subgroups of five, multivariate charts watching
    correlated dimensions simultaneously, and machine-learning models hunting patterns the
    Western Electric rules never anticipated. The constants through all of it are Shewhart's:
    know your noise, listen for the signal, and never mistake one for the other.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Industrial &amp; Systems specifications; Montgomery, <em>Introduction to Statistical Quality Control</em>; Shewhart, <em>Economic Control of Quality</em> (1931).</p>
</TopicPage>
